+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface
+
+**Feature**: `001-logredact-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-20
+
+This is the tool's only external interface. It is a text-protocol filter: operands and options in,
+redacted text on stdout, diagnostics on stderr, meaning in the exit status. Everything stated
+here is asserted by a test in `tests/test_cli.py`.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [-h | --help] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` and CPython 3.11 or newer. No installation step.
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count app.log
+```
+
+## Operands
+
+| Operand | Cardinality | Meaning |
+|---------|-------------|---------|
+| `FILE` | zero or more | A path to read. Files are read in the order given (FR-003). With no `FILE`, standard input is read instead (FR-002). |
+
+- A `FILE` of `-` is a literal relative path to a file named `-`. It does **not** mean standard
+  input.
+- Operands and options may be interleaved in any order — `logredact a.log --count b.log` is
+  equivalent to `logredact --count a.log b.log` — and `--count` is never consumed as a `FILE`
+  (FR-001).
+- Named files are opened read-only and never modified.
+
+## Options
+
+| Option | Effect |
+|--------|--------|
+| `--count` | After all output is written and flushed, write one tally line to stderr (FR-023, FR-024). |
+| `-h`, `--help` | Write usage and option descriptions to stdout and exit 0 without opening any input source, without writing a tally even if `--count` was also given, and without writing anything to stderr (FR-030a). |
+
+Any other option is a usage error: see Exit status 2 below. There are no options to change the
+placeholder text or to enable/disable a category.
+
+## Standard input
+
+Read only when no `FILE` operand is present, and never on a help run. Decoded as UTF-8 with
+undecodable bytes preserved (see Encoding).
+
+## Standard output
+
+One concatenated stream: the redacted contents of each source, in source order, with each
+source's lines in their original order (FR-004).
+
+| Guarantee | Requirement |
+|-----------|-------------|
+| Every character not part of a match appears unchanged, in position | FR-005 |
+| Line terminators are reproduced exactly; only `\n` ends a line, a bare `\r` is ordinary text, `\r\n` is written back as `\r\n`, and mixed endings each survive | FR-007 |
+| A missing final newline stays missing; a present one stays present | FR-007a |
+| Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are reproduced byte for byte | FR-005a |
+| No diagnostic is ever written here | FR-031 |
+
+On a help run, stdout carries the usage text and nothing else.
+
+### Redactions
+
+| Input | Output | Counted as |
+|-------|--------|------------|
+| Email address | `[EMAIL]` | `emails` |
+| IPv4 address | `[IP]` | `ips` |
+| Luhn-valid 13–19 digit card number | `[CARD]` | `cards` |
+
+Recognition rules — what is and is not a match — are specified in
+[redaction-patterns.md](./redaction-patterns.md). Each character of a line belongs to at most one
+redaction; the earliest-starting match wins, and at the same start position the order is email,
+then IPv4, then card (FR-020, FR-021). Placeholder text already in the input is passed through
+unchanged and uncounted (FR-022).
+
+## Standard error
+
+| Condition | Content |
+|-----------|---------|
+| `--count`, successful run | Exactly `emails=N ips=N cards=N\n` (FR-023) |
+| No `--count`, successful run | Empty (FR-025) |
+| Help run | Empty (FR-030a) |
+| Unreadable `FILE` | One diagnostic containing that path as spelled on the command line (FR-028); **no** tally line, even with `--count` (FR-029) |
+| Invalid usage | argparse usage line plus the error (FR-030) |
+| Failed write to stdout | One concise diagnostic that output could not be written; no tally line (FR-031a) |
+
+Redacted log content is never written here (FR-031). No failure path emits a traceback or other
+unhandled-error text (FR-031a, SC-005).
+
+Diagnostic form (prefix is stable; the trailing detail is the OS message and is not asserted
+verbatim by tests):
+
+```text
+logredact: error: cannot read 'missing.log': [Errno 2] No such file or directory
+logredact: error: failed writing to standard output
+```
+
+## Exit status
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|--------|---------|
+| `0` | Every named source was read to completion **and** all output was written and flushed successfully (FR-027); or `--help` was requested (FR-030a) |
+| `2` | A source could not be opened or read (FR-028); or usage was invalid (FR-030); or output could not be fully written (FR-031a) |
+
+Status `1` is never produced. A run whose output was truncated never exits `0` — which is what
+lets a reviewer tell a fully-processed log from a partial one without inspecting the log (SC-009).
+
+## Encoding
+
+Input and output are UTF-8 with `errors="surrogateescape"`. A byte that is not valid UTF-8 is
+carried through to the output unchanged; it never causes a failure, never produces a replacement
+character, never matches a redaction category, and never counts toward the tally. Text
+surrounding such a byte is redacted normally, and a digit run interrupted by one is not a
+contiguous card candidate (FR-005a).
+
+## Ordering and buffering
+
+1. All redacted output is written to stdout.
+2. stdout is flushed. A failure here is a failed write (exit 2, no tally).
+3. Only then is the tally written to stderr, if `--count` was given.
+
+A consumer redirecting the two streams separately therefore always sees a complete log before the
+summary.
+
+## Behaviour under a closing reader
+
+`python -m logredact big.log | head -1` is a **failure**, not a success: `head` exits and closes
+the pipe, the next write or the final flush fails, and the tool writes its one-line diagnostic to
+stderr and exits 2 with no tally and no traceback (FR-031a). SIGPIPE is left at CPython's
+inherited `SIG_IGN` so the failure arrives as an exception rather than killing the process.
+
+## Worked examples
+
+All three reproduce byte-exactly (SC-001).
+
+```bash
+$ printf 'user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact
+user [EMAIL] from [IP]
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+```bash
+$ printf 'paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact
+paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+```bash
+$ printf 'a@b.io 1.2.3.4\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count
+[EMAIL] [IP]          # stdout
+emails=1 ips=1 cards=0  # stderr
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+## Internal entry point
+
+`logredact.cli.main(argv: list[str]) -> int` is importable and returns the exit status rather than
+calling `sys.exit`, so `__main__.py` is a one-liner and tests can call it directly. This is an
+implementation convenience, not a supported library API: the package exports nothing from
+`__init__.py` and the CLI above is the contract.
