+# Contract: redaction recognition grammar
+
+**Feature**: `001-logredact-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-20
+
+What the tool treats as personal data, and what it deliberately leaves alone. This is as much a
+part of the external contract as the command line: a user deciding whether a redacted log is safe
+to share needs to know precisely which forms are caught. Every row in the "must not match" tables
+is a look-alike the specification names, and each has a test.
+
+All character classes are ASCII. `[0-9]`, `[A-Za-z]`, and the separator class never match
+non-ASCII digits or letters, and never match a surrogate standing in for an undecodable byte.
+
+---
+
+## Scanning model
+
+One compiled alternation is searched repeatedly across each line:
+
+```text
+(?P<email>EMAIL) | (?P<ip>IPV4) | (?P<card>CARD)
+```
+
+- The match found is the one at the **leftmost** position where any alternative matches.
+- At that position, alternatives are tried in written order: email, then IPv4, then card
+  (FR-021).
+- An accepted match's span is replaced by its placeholder; scanning resumes at the span's end.
+  Placeholder text is never rescanned (FR-022).
+- The card alternative — and only the card alternative — may be **rejected after matching**, by
+  the Luhn check. On rejection nothing is replaced and scanning resumes one character after the
+  candidate's start, so a later valid candidate is still found (FR-019a).
+- Matches never overlap or nest (FR-020). Matching is per line; a candidate split across a line
+  boundary is not recognized.
+
+---
+
+## Email → `[EMAIL]`
+
+```text
+local   = [A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+
+host    = (?:[A-Za-z0-9-]+\.)+ [A-Za-z]{2,}
+octet   = (?:25[0-5] | 2[0-4][0-9] | [01][0-9][0-9] | [0-9][0-9] | [0-9])
+quad    = octet (?:\. octet){3}
+EMAIL   = local @ (?: host | quad (?![0-9.]) )
+```
+
+| Must match (one `[EMAIL]`, counted once) | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `jane@example.com` | FR-009 base form |
+| `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` | dots and `+` in the local part, multi-label domain; the **whole** address is replaced, no fragment left behind (FR-009, FR-010) |
+| `a@b.io` | shortest form: single-character local part, two-letter final label |
+| `admin@1.2.3.4` | FR-021's worked example — one `[EMAIL]`, not `admin@[IP]`. See the amendment note below. |
+| `1234567890123@example.com` | both the email and the card alternative can start at offset 0; email wins (FR-021) |
+
+| Must not match | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `admin@999.1.1.1` | unchanged — the domain is neither a hostname with a 2+ letter final label nor a valid dotted quad, and `999.1.1.1` is not an IPv4 address either |
+| `jane@localhost` | unchanged — a single-label domain |
+| `jane@example` | unchanged — fewer than two labels |
+
+A trailing dot is not part of the match: `jane@example.com.` yields `[EMAIL].`.
+
+> **Amendment note (research item [R-000](../research.md)).** FR-009 requires the final domain
+> label to be two or more *letters*, which excludes `1.2.3.4`; FR-021 and the Edge Cases both
+> require `admin@1.2.3.4` to produce exactly one `[EMAIL]`. The two cannot both hold. The `quad`
+> alternative above is the resolution: it admits an IPv4-literal domain so the full address is
+> redacted and the local part is not left exposed. This is the single place where the delivered
+> behaviour departs from a literal reading of the spec text, and reversing it means deleting the
+> `| quad (?![0-9.])` alternative.
+
+---
+
+## IPv4 → `[IP]`
+
+```text
+IPV4 = (?<![0-9.]) octet (?:\. octet){3} (?![0-9.])
+```
+
+Exactly four dot-separated groups, each with a numeric value of 0–255. Leading zeros are accepted
+because the value is what matters, so `010.0.0.1` and `00.0.0.0` are addresses (spec
+Assumptions). The lookarounds implement FR-014: a candidate immediately preceded or followed by a
+digit or a dot is not an address.
+
+| Must match | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `192.168.0.10`, `10.0.0.7`, `1.2.3.4` | FR-012 |
+| `0.0.0.0`, `255.255.255.255` | range endpoints |
+| `010.0.0.1` | leading zero, value in range |
+
+| Must not match (output unchanged) | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `999.1.1.1` | first group exceeds 255 (FR-013) |
+| `256.1.1.1` | first group exceeds 255 |
+| `1.2.3.4.5` | the quad is immediately followed by a dot (FR-014) |
+| `1.2.3.45678` | the quad is immediately followed by a digit (FR-014) |
+| `1.2.3` | only three groups |
+| `1.2.3.4.5.6` | no four-group span survives the lookarounds |
+| `v1.2.3.4` | *does* match the `1.2.3.4` — `v` is neither a digit nor a dot, so this yields `v[IP]` |
+
+---
+
+## Payment card → `[CARD]`
+
+```text
+sep       = [ \-]                       (one space or one hyphen)
+CARD      = (?<![0-9]) [0-9] (?:sep?[0-9]){12,18} (?![0-9])
+```
+
+A candidate is 13–19 digits, optionally written in groups separated by a *single* space or
+hyphen, beginning and ending with a digit, with no two separators adjacent, and not flanked by a
+digit. A candidate is redacted **only if its digits pass the Luhn checksum** (FR-017).
+
+**Luhn**: walking the digits right to left, double every second digit; subtract 9 from any
+product over 9; sum all values; the candidate is valid when the sum is a multiple of 10.
+
+**Selection at a start position** (FR-019): the candidate lengths 19, 18, … 13 are tried at the
+start position and the **longest Luhn-valid** one is redacted. If none is valid, the scan resumes
+one character later (FR-019a).
+
+| Must match | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `4111111111111111` | 16 digits, unseparated, Luhn-valid (FR-016) |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111` | single-space groups |
+| `4111-1111-1111-1111` | single-hyphen groups |
+| `4111-1111 1111-1111` | mixed single separators are allowed |
+
+| Must not match / partial behaviour | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `1234567890123` | unchanged — 13 digits, Luhn fails (FR-017) |
+| a 12-digit run | unchanged — too short, even if it would pass Luhn |
+| an unbroken run of 20+ digits | **entirely** unchanged, even when a 13–19 digit stretch inside it passes Luhn; length disqualifies the run and the checksum is never consulted to rescue part of it (FR-018) |
+| `4111  1111  1111  1111` (doubled spaces) | unchanged — doubled separators break the run into 4-digit pieces, all too short |
+| `99 4111 1111 1111 1111` | → `99 [CARD]`. The 18-digit candidate at offset 0 fails Luhn (sum 48), as does the 14-digit one (sum 42); the 16 digits after the space pass, so they are redacted and `99 ` is copied through (FR-019a) |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111, ref` | → `[CARD], ref`. A redaction never begins or ends on a separator, so trailing punctuation survives |
+| `4111 1111 1111 11x1` | unchanged — `x` is not a separator, so the only candidate is the 14-digit prefix `4111 1111 1111 11` (Luhn sum 27, fails); every later start is flanked by a digit or yields fewer than 13 digits |
+
+A digit run interrupted by an undecodable byte is not contiguous and so is not a single candidate
+(FR-005a).
+
+---
+
+## Never matched by any category
+
+| Input | Reason |
+|---|---|
+| `[EMAIL]`, `[IP]`, `[CARD]` already in the input | passed through unchanged and uncounted (FR-022) |
+| IPv6 addresses | out of scope (spec Assumptions) |
+| Names, phone numbers, MAC addresses, URLs, usernames, session tokens | out of scope — exactly three categories are redacted |
+| Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 | cannot match an ASCII class (FR-005a) |
+| Anything spanning a line boundary | matching is per line |
+
+---
+
+## Counting
+
+Each accepted match increments its category by one, so a line with three addresses contributes
+`emails=3` (FR-026). Rejected card candidates increment nothing. The totals therefore equal the
+number of placeholders of each category in the output stream, which is how SC-008 is verified.
